hadiths[205]{hadithnumber,text}:
993,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 993 Arabic Hadees: 89
994,"On the authority of Ibn Abbas, he said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), when he wanted to relieve himself, would go far away. So he went out one day for his need, then performed ablution and put on one of his two leather socks. Then a green bird came, took the other leather sock, flew up with it, then dropped it. A black snake came out of it. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'A karamah (honor) with which Allah has honored me.' Then the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the evil of him who walks on his belly, and from the evil of him who walks on two legs, and from the evil of him who walks on four.'"" Narrated by al-Tabarani in al-Awsat, and it contains Sa'd ibn Tarif, who was accused of fabrication."
995,"On the authority of Bilāl ibn al-Ḥārith, who said: ""We went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on one of his journeys. He went out to relieve himself—and when he went out to relieve himself, he would go far away. I brought him a vessel of water. He went, and I heard a dispute among men and a clamor the likes of which I had never heard. He came back and said: 'Bilāl?' I said: 'Here I am, Bilāl.' He said: 'Do you have water with you?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'You have done well,' and he took it from me and performed ablution. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I heard a dispute among men and a clamor near you, the likes of which I have never heard from their tongues.' He said: 'The Muslim jinn and the polytheist jinn disputed before me. They asked me to settle them, so I settled the Muslims in al-Jalas, and I settled the polytheists in al-Ghawr.'""I said to Kathīr: ""What is al-Jalas? And what is al-Ghawr?"" He said: ""Al-Jalas: the villages and the mountains. Al-Ghawr: what is between the mountains and the seas.""Kathīr said: ""We have never seen anyone afflicted in al-Jalas except that he was safe, nor anyone afflicted in al-Ghawr except that he hardly survived.""I said: Ibn Mājah narrated from him: ""When he wanted to relieve himself, he would go far away, that is all.""In its chain is Kathīr ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf, and they have unanimously agreed on his weakness, though al-Tirmidhī graded his hadith as ḥasan."
996,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 996 Arabic Hadees: 179
