1021,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1021 Arabic Hadees: 743
1022,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1022 Arabic Hadees: 744
1023,"On the authority of ʿĀʾishah, she said: ""The Prophet — may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him — passed by two graves whose occupants were being punished, so he said: 'Indeed they are being punished, and they are not being punished for something major. One of them used not to protect himself from urine, and the other used to walk about spreading slander.' Then he called for a fresh palm branch, broke it in two, and placed one piece on this grave and the other on that grave, and said: 'Perhaps it will lighten their punishment until they dry out.'""Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Awsaṭ, and its narrators are trustworthy, except for al-Ṭabarānī's shaykh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Wākiʿī al-Miṣrī, for I did not know him.And other ḥadīths of this nature concerning the punishment of the grave will follow."
1024,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1024 Arabic Hadees: 754
1025,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1025 Arabic Hadees: 756
1026,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1026 Arabic Hadees: 758
1027,"On the authority of Abu Bakrah, he said: ""While the Prophet - may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him - was walking between me and another man, he came upon two graves. He said: 'Indeed, the occupants of these two graves are being punished.' Then he said: 'Bring me a palm branch.' Abu Bakrah said: So I and my companion raced to bring him a palm branch. He split it into two halves, placed one in this grave and one in that grave. He said: 'Perhaps it will lighten their punishment as long as they remain fresh. Indeed, they are being punished for something not major: backbiting and urine.'"" Narrated by Al-Tabarani in Al-Awsat, and Ahmad, and this is the wording of Al-Tabarani. And Ahmad said: ""And they are not being punished for something major; rather, they are being punished only for backbiting and urine."" And Ibn Majah narrated it in abbreviated form, and its narrators are trustworthy."
1028,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1028 Arabic Hadees: 811
1029,"On the authority of Abu Umamah, who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) passed by on a day of intense heat towards Baqi' al-Gharqad. The people were walking behind him. When he heard the sound of their sandals, it weighed upon his soul, so he sat down until he put them ahead of him, lest pride enter his heart. Then when he passed by Baqi' al-Gharqad, behold, there were two graves in which two men had been buried. The Prophet (peace be upon him) stopped and said: ""Who did you bury here today?"" They said: So-and-so and so-and-so. They said: O Messenger of Allah, and what is that? He said: ""As for one of them, he used not to protect himself from urine, and as for the other, he used to walk with tale-bearing."" Then he took a fresh palm branch, split it, and placed it on the two graves. They said: O Messenger of Allah, why did you do that? He said: ""So that their punishment may be lightened for them."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, until when will they be punished? He said: ""The unseen, none knows it except Allah."" He said: ""And were it not for the breaking of your hearts and your excessive talking, you would have heard what I hear."" Narrated by Ahmad, and in its chain is Ali ibn Yazid [ibn Ali] al-Ahani from al-Qasim, and both of them are weak."
1030,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1030 Arabic Hadees: 849
1031,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1031 Arabic Hadees: 850
1032,"On the authority of Shufayy ibn Matīʿ al-Aṣbaḥī, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he said: ""Four [types of people] harm the inhabitants of the Fire despite the suffering they already endure, hastening between scalding water and the Blazing Fire, crying out for woe and destruction. The inhabitants of the Fire say to one another: 'What is the matter with these [four] that they have harmed us on top of the harm we are in?'"" He said: ""A man locked inside a coffin of burning embers, a man dragging his intestines, a man whose mouth flows with pus and blood, and a man eating his own flesh."" He said: ""It is said to the owner of the coffin: 'What is the matter with the distant one that he has harmed us on top of the harm we are in?'"" He said: ""He says: 'The distant one died while owing people's wealth, unable to find a way to settle or fulfill it.'"" Then he said to the one dragging his intestines: ""What is the matter with the distant one that he has harmed us on top of the harm we are in?"" He said: ""The distant one used not to care where urine touched him, not washing it off."" Then he said to the one whose mouth flows with pus and blood: ""What is the matter with the distant one that he has harmed us on top of the harm we are in?"" He says: ""The distant one used to eat the flesh of people [i.e., backbite them]."" Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Muʿjam al-Kabīr, and it is thus in the heard original, and its narrators are trustworthy."
1033,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1033 Arabic Hadees: 857
1034,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1034 Arabic Hadees: 858
1035,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1035 Arabic Hadees: 861
1036,"On the authority of Abu Musa, he said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) urinating while sitting, having separated his thighs widely, until I felt pity for him due to the length of his sitting. Then he came [back] holding sixty-three [pebbles] in his hand, and he said: 'Verily, the companion of the Children of Israel was stricter regarding urine than you; he had a pair of scissors, so whenever something of urine touched his garment, he would cut it off.'""Al-Tabarani narrated it in *Al-Mu'jam Al-Kabir* — and he has a hadith in the *Sahih* other than this — and in its chain is Ali ibn Asim, who used to make many mistakes and errors; when his error was pointed out to him, he would not recant, and he belittled the memorizers (huffaz)."
1037,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1037 Arabic Hadees: 869
1038,"And from Al-Zubayr ibn Al-'Awwam, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) led us in the Fajr prayer in the Mosque of Medina. When he finished, he said: 'Which of you will follow me tonight to the delegation of the jinn?' The people remained silent, and none of them spoke. He repeated this three times. Then he passed by me while walking, took my hand, and I walked with him until all the mountains of Medina disappeared from view, and we reached a barren plain. There were tall men, like spears, with their garments tucked up between their legs. When I saw them, a severe trembling overcame me, so that my legs could not hold me from fear. When we drew near to them, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) drew a line for me on the ground with the thumb of his foot, and said to me: 'Sit in the middle of it.' When I sat down, everything I had been feeling of dread vanished. The Prophet (peace be upon him) went forward between me and them, and recited the Qur'an in a raised voice until dawn broke. Then he returned and passed by me, saying: 'Come along.' So I walked with him. We had not gone far when he said to me: 'Turn around and look, do you see anyone where those people were?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I see a great black mass.' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) lowered his head to the ground, picked up a bone with a piece of dung, and threw it to them. Then he said: 'Their guidance is from me. They are a delegation of a people, the delegation of Nusaybin. They asked me for provisions, so I assigned to them every bone and piece of dung.' Al-Zubayr said: 'Therefore, it is not permissible for anyone to use a bone or dung for istinja' (cleaning after relieving oneself) ever.' Narrated by Al-Tabarani in Al-Mu'jam Al-Kabir, and its chain is good (hasan), containing no one but Baqiyyah, and he explicitly stated he heard it directly."
1039,"Abdullah ibn Mas'ud said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) asked me to accompany him one night, and he said: 'A group of jinn — fifteen, brothers and cousins — are coming to me tonight, so I will recite the Quran to them.' So I went with him to the place he intended, and he drew a line for me and made me sit, and said: 'Do not leave this [line].' So I spent the night in it until the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came to me at dawn, and in his hand was a dry bone, a piece of dung, and a piece of charcoal. He said: 'When you go to relieve yourself, do not clean yourself with any of these.' He said: 'When morning came, I said: I will find out where the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was. So I went and saw the tracks of seventy camels.'""Al-Tabarani narrated it in Al-Awsat, and in its chain is Abdullah ibn Salih, the scribe of Al-Layth. The imams, including Ahmad and others, weakened him, while Yahya ibn Ma'in and Abd al-Malik ibn Shu'ayb ibn Al-Layth authenticated him. The rest of its narrators are narrators of the Sahih.And Abdullah has a lengthy hadith coming in the Signs of Prophecy, narrated by Ahmad."
1040,Al-Mu'jam al-Saghir Tabarani Hadees: 1040 Arabic Hadees: 893
